  "notes": "Arista diva on Shep Crawford and Montell Jordan ballad production, with a parallel house-remix identity, 1995–2002. The research documents both halves — \"**60–65 half-time** originals (backbeat on 3) and a documented **128–132 four-on-the-floor** remix twin\" — and the originals are what is encoded, because the remix \"reharmonises nothing and simply re-beds the vocal\": it is a different record's kit under the same song, not a second grammar of hers. Three drum deltas from the originals: \"kick-on-1\" at one or two hits a bar with syncopation at 0.05, \"almost no hats\" as an 8th lane at 0.03 fill density, and \"a huge snare reverb\" — which has no schema field, so what is encoded is its consequence, a snare sitting 4–10 ms behind the grid with almost no ghost activity and a percussion bed at −11 dB. Harmony is the entry's strongest claim: \"gospel-diva writing — 4-chord minor cycles, ii–V turnarounds into the chorus\", so `ii–v–i` shares top weight with the four-chord minor family and the harmonic rhythm moves once a bar rather than vamping. \"A full-belt climax a 9th or 10th above the tonic, and heavy melisma in the final third\" is a twenty-eight-semitone melody register with a 3–10 density and the highest arch weight in this batch. The bass is \"sustained synth bass on roots on the originals\", so no `bass808` block and a single-degree cell. ⚠ The research marks the key claim `[LOW-CONF]`, so the keys are lane-level. ⚠ Every velocity and density number is INFERRED.",
